🔍 FUNCTIONAL BEHAVIOR NOTES
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

• raid1, raid1c3, and raid1c4 adapt dynamically to drive count.
  If fewer drives exist than needed for full replication,
  Btrfs stores as many copies as possible and warns the user.

• Add drives anytime and run:
    btrfs balance start -dconvert=raid1c4 -mconvert=raid1c4 /mnt/fs
  to upgrade data & metadata redundancy automatically.

• raid10 requires an even number of devices; creation fails otherwise.
• raid1c4 works with any count ≥4 (4, 5, 6, 7...) and always keeps 4 copies.
• Redundancy increases space usage proportionally:
  - raid1 → 2× space
  - raid1c3 → 3× space
  - raid1c4 → 4× space

Recommended quick logic:
  - ≤2 drives → raid1
  - 3 drives → raid1c3
  - 4+ drives → raid10 or raid1c4
  - 5+ drives → raid1c4 preferred
  - large arrays → raid6 (only for non-critical data)

